  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA passing cold front across central Ohio this afternoon and\nevening will support isolated showers and thunderstorms across\nthe region. With dry air noted aloft on satellite, some \nupdrafts may struggle to reach 20-24kft layer, where the theta-e\nminimums could promote the development of cooler, stronger \ndowndrafts. However, any storm able to reach these heights could\nbriefly produce isolated damaging wind gusts. The greatest \npotential for this threat is expected between 3pm and 8pm east\nof Pittsburgh. The threat for heavy rainfall remains very low. \nStorm chances will decrease between 9pm and 11pm tonight, though\nlingering low- level moisture may support areas of fog \novernight into early Friday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nQuiet and drier weather is expected Friday through Sunday under\nhigh pressure. \n\nLong-range guidance remains consistent in indicating a\nsignificant pattern shift Sunday into Monday, as a trough\ndevelops along the West Coast and promotes strengthening ridge\nacross the Great Lakes. This evolution will favor a return to\nhot and humid conditions, with temperatures expected to climb\ninto the upper-80s and near 90 degrees beginning Monday and\ncontinuing through Wednesday. However, the probability of any \nheat products is considered low. \n\nThe eventual breakdown of the Great Lakes ridge will be a key\nfactor in determining the potential for severe weather across\nthe region next week. Machine-learning guidance suggests\nprobabilities may begin to increasing as early as Tuesday\nafternoon, currently ranging from 5-15%, with a more notable\nincrease expected Wednesday to around 15-30%.",
